<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Danish West India and Guinea Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1671–1776</span> · Also known as Danish West India Company</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Danish West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> Company was a Danish chartered company founded in 1671 to run colonies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and trade on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. It administered St Thomas, St John and, from 1733, St Croix, bought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, as well as forts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osu-castle">Christiansborg</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>. It shipped enslaved Africans to its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations until the crown took over its possessions in 1754–1776.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/danish-west-india-and-guinea-company.jpg" alt="Danish West India and Guinea Company" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vestindisk-guineisk_Kompagnis_flag.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
